WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.
我是珍娜·费舍尔。

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.
我是安吉拉·金赛。

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.
我们曾一起出演《办公室》。

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.
我们是最好的朋友。

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.
现在，我们专门为你制作了这档《办公室》终极重温播客。

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.
每周，我们都会深度解析一集《办公室》，并分享只有身临其境的我们才能告诉你的独家幕后故事。

7
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office ladies.
我们是《办公室》女士。

8
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.870
Hey, buddy.
嘿，伙计。

9
00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.490
Good morning, everyone.
大家早上好。

10
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.430
I always say good morning.
我总是说早上好。

11
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:29.750
It's because we're here in the morning.
那是因为我们是在早上录制。

12
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:30.710
Because we're here in the morning.
因为我们是在早上录制。

13
00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:37.230
Every time I do it, Jenna gives me this look like, Angela, it might not be morning for everyone else.
每次我这么说，珍娜都会用那种眼神看着我，好像在说：安吉拉，对其他人来说可能不是早上。

14
00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:38.330
Don't stop.
别停。

15
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.710
Don't stop saying good morning.
别停止说早上好。

16
00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.570
Should we talk about the episode we're going to be breaking down today?
我们要聊聊今天打算解析的那一集吗？

17
00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.070
I would love nothing more.
我再乐意不过了。

18
00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.430
I found this episode hilarious.
我觉得这一集太搞笑了。

19
00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.650
This snuck up on me.
这集真是出乎我的意料。

20
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:50.130
Yes.
没错。

21
00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:51.410
It was a sneaker-upper.
它是个惊喜。

22
00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.270
I absolutely loved it.
我真的太喜欢了。

23
00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:59.290
You know, you look back and you think about The Office and all the, like, sort of obvious ones come to mind.
你知道，当你回首《办公室》时，脑海里浮现的通常是那些显而易见的经典集数。

24
00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.690
But this is so incredibly good.
但这一集真的好得不可思议。

25
00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.590
It should be on all the lists.
它应该出现在所有的榜单上。

26
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:05.370
It should.
确实应该。

27
00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.490
And there was more to it that didn't make it in that is so delicious.
而且还有更多没能放进正片的精彩内容，非常有趣。

28
00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:10.970
And I can't wait to share that.
我迫不及待想分享给大家了。

29
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.730
What we're talking about is Broke.
我们要聊的是《破产》这一集。

30
00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:21.330
Season 5, episode 25, written by Charlie Grandy and directed by our very own Steve Carell.
第五季第25集，由查理·格兰迪编剧，我们自己的史蒂夫·卡瑞尔执导。

31
00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:22.890
How fun was that?
那有多好玩？

32
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:24.370
That was amazing.
那太棒了。

33
00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.350
That is my fastback number one.
那是我的第一快讯。

34
00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:26.970
Ooh, do it.
噢，快说。

35
00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.550
But we should start with a summary.
但我们应该先从剧情摘要开始。

36
00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:28.890
Oh, we should.
噢，我们应该的。

37
00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:29.850
I need my order.
我需要我的流程。

38
00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:30.490
Go and order.
去按流程来。

39
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:30.910
Okay.
好的。

40
00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.150
I need to say good morning.
我需要说早上好。

41
00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:33.270
You need to go and order.
你需要去按流程来。

42
00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:34.310
It's who we are.
这就是我们的风格。

43
00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:34.910
It's who we are.
这就是我们的风格。

44
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:36.670
All right.
好吧。

45
00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:46.770
The summary is, exhausted from making their own deliveries, the Michael Scott Paper Company is steadily gaining clients due to their very low prices,
剧情摘要是：迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司因亲自送货而精疲力竭，由于价格极低，他们的客户正在稳步增加，

46
00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.650
but it turns out they're going broke in the process.
但结果发现他们在这个过程中正面临破产。

47
00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:50.670
Yeah.
没错。

48
00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:59.490
Meanwhile, Dunder Mifflin is desperate to negotiate a buyout because they have lost several important clients to Michael Scott Paper Company.
与此同时，邓德·米夫林公司急于协商收购，因为他们已经失去了几个重要客户给迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司。

49
00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:01.450
But Michael has other ideas.
但迈克尔有别的想法。

50
00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:06.870
I was so impressed and also scared by all of Michael's tactics.
迈克尔的所有策略都让我印象深刻，同时也让我感到害怕。

51
00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:14.450
When he gets out of the elevator and he's like doubled over and he's like, I'm really afraid I'm going to say we're broke.
当他走出电梯，整个人弯着腰，他说，我真的很怕我会说我们破产了。

52
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:14.990
I'm not going to be able to do it.
我做不到。

53
00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.390
It's so funny.
太搞笑了。

54
00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:17.510
All right.
好吧。

55
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:21.090
Fastback number one, Steve Carell directed this episode.
第一个快讯，史蒂夫·卡瑞尔执导了这一集。

56
00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:26.890
It was his first time directing the show and his first time directing in general.
这是他第一次执导这部剧，也是他第一次担任导演。

57
00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.370
He was so prepared.
他准备得非常充分。

58
00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:29.850
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

59
00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:30.350
Yes.
是的。

60
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.090
I was so impressed with him.
我对他印象非常深刻。

61
00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:40.690
We talked to Randy Cordray about that, and he said Steve's work ethic was incredible, that he was totally prepared.
我们和兰迪·科德雷聊过这个，他说史蒂夫的职业道德令人难以置信，他完全做好了准备。

62
00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:43.570
He had great ideas, great energy.
他有很多很棒的想法，精力充沛。

63
00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.550
He said he was so respectful of every crew position.
他说史蒂夫非常尊重每一个剧组岗位。

64
00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:52.230
He wanted everyone's input to make the scenes better.
他希望听取每个人的意见，让场景变得更好。

65
00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:57.190
He said Steve totally immersed himself in the prep of this episode.
他说史蒂夫完全沉浸在这一集的筹备工作中。

66
00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.630
I loved him as a director.
我非常喜欢他作为导演的表现。

67
00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:06.970
I have one little moment I'll share when we get there of a note he gave me that I was like, totally changed the scene for me.
我有一个小插曲，等讲到那儿的时候我会分享，他给我的一个建议彻底改变了我对那场戏的理解。

68
00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.890
Well, I traded some messages with Steve about this episode.
嗯，我跟史蒂夫就这一集互通了一些信息。

69
00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:15.490
I just wanted to know, Steve, what was that like for you to direct all of us?
我只是想知道，史蒂夫，执导我们所有人是什么感觉？

70
00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:20.450
Because he was, I believe, the first cast member to step into a directing role.
因为我相信他是第一个担任导演角色的剧组成员。

71
00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:21.770
Oh, I think you're right.
哦，我想你是对的。

72
00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.270
And here's what Steve said.
这是史蒂夫的原话。

73
00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:23.990
This is a quote.
这是一段引语。

74
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:28.050
It was like being the conductor of a world-class orchestra.
就像是在指挥一支世界级的管弦乐队。

75
00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:28.610
Aw.
噢。

76
00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.070
Everyone was so good.
每个人都太棒了。

77
00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:31.210
It was pretty easy.
这其实挺容易的。

78
00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:31.950
Aw.
噢。

79
00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:41.410
Well, we got a fan question from Bridget R., who said, what was it like having a main cast member direct, and did it present any challenges?
我们收到一个粉丝布里奇特·R的问题，她问，由主要演员担任导演是什么感觉，这有没有带来什么挑战？

80
00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:44.390
Well, a little bit.
嗯，有一点。

81
00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:52.210
Because, you know, Steve couldn't watch the scene when he was performing in the scene.
因为，你知道，史蒂夫在表演的时候没法监看镜头。

82
00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.050
Right, right.
没错，没错。

83
00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:59.490
And the week before you direct an episode, the director normally preps the episode,
通常在执导一集的前一周，导演会进行筹备工作，

84
00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:09.250
and that sort of involves meeting with each of the different department heads to make decisions about the locations, about the props, the wardrobe, camera setups.
这涉及到与各个部门主管开会，对拍摄地点、道具、服装、摄像机位做出决定。

85
00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:15.810
And this was a little tricky for Steve because Steve spent the prior week acting in an episode.
这对史蒂夫来说有点棘手，因为他前一周还在忙着演另一集。

86
00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:25.310
I remember he had to sort of squeeze things in, like he'd be in a scene, and then he would have to, like, hop into, like, a van to go location scout or.
我记得他必须挤时间，比如他刚演完一场戏，就得跳上一辆面包车去勘景，或者做别的事。

87
00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:25.810
Yes.
是的。

88
00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:26.110
Yeah.
没错。

89
00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:34.110
Randy said, luckily, the Michael Scott Paper Company storyline was such that Steve generally had a couple days off each week.
兰迪说，幸运的是，迈克尔·斯科特造纸公司的剧情线让史蒂夫每周通常有几天休息时间。

90
00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:34.590
Yeah.
是的。

91
00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:40.350
For when we would shoot up at Dunder Mifflin, and so he immersed himself in prep for those two days.
在我们拍摄邓德·米福林办公室戏份的时候，他就会利用那两天全身心投入筹备工作。

92
00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:41.650
But it's true.
但这是真的。

93
00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:49.490
I would notice there would be this van waiting outside at lunch, and they would go scout a location for a half hour.
我注意到午餐时外面总停着一辆面包车，他们会去勘景半小时。

94
00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:49.970
Yeah.
是的。

95
00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.090
And then he'd come back and jump into scenes.
然后他回来又接着拍戏。

96
00:04:52.350 --> 00:05:05.250
Well, I also spoke with Randall Einhorn, our cinematographer, who said he remembers Steve was very focused on characters and actor performance as a director.
我还和我们的摄影指导兰德尔·艾因霍恩聊过，他说他记得史蒂夫作为导演非常关注角色和演员的表演。

97
00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.270
So I guess this will come up in the note that he gave you, Angela.
所以我猜这会出现在他给你的建议里，安吉拉。

98
00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:08.710
Yeah.
是的。

99
00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:10.930
But he said he was just so respectful.
但他确实非常尊重人。

100
00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:13.190
He was so devoid of any ego.
他完全没有一点架子。

101
00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:16.950
And that's how I remember working with Steve.
这就是我对与史蒂夫共事的记忆。

102
00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:19.770
He just made you better naturally.
他自然而然地让你变得更优秀。

103
00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.990
He's just someone that makes everything better.
他就是那种能让一切变得更好的人。

104
00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:23.910
Yeah.
没错。

105
00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:25.810
You know, you know those people.
你知道的，你认识那样的人。

106
00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:29.010
You're like, gosh, I feel so great whenever I'm around them.
你会觉得，天哪，只要和他们在一起，我就感觉特别好。

107
00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.130
I took this leadership seminar once.
我曾经参加过一个领导力研讨会。

108
00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:47.850
And they said that a great leader is a person where, like, you leave your interaction with that person, and you feel more confident and better, and you notice yourself more.
他们说，伟大的领导者是这样的人：当你与他交流结束离开时，你会感到更自信、状态更好，也更关注自我。

109
00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:52.370
That that is your goal if you're ever in a leadership position.
如果你身处领导岗位，这就是你的目标。

110
00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:56.430
Not for you to show off how interesting and powerful you are.
而不是为了炫耀你自己有多么有趣、多么强大。

111
00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.550
But to try to empower other people.
而是要努力去赋能他人。

112
00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:01.070
Well, that's definitely Steve.
嗯，那绝对就是史蒂夫。

113
00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:02.550
That's definitely Steve.
那绝对就是史蒂夫。

114
00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:03.870
Guys, yeah.
伙计们，没错。

115
00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:07.150
I'm that person who went to leadership seminars on the weekends.
我就是那种周末会去参加领导力研讨会的人。

116
00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:08.890
I wasn't going to say anything.
我本来不想说的。

117
00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:09.510
I'm that gal.
我就是那种人。

118
00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:10.990
I wasn't going to say anything.
我本来不想说的。

119
00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:12.510
Sign me up.
算我一个。

120
00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:17.050
As you said it, I thought, oh, I would have just loved to have known Jenna then.
听你这么一说，我在想，噢，我真希望那时候就认识珍娜。

121
00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.810
I saw this thing someone posted on Instagram.
我看到有人在 Instagram 上发了条内容。

122
00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:21.230
Jenna, it made me laugh so hard.
珍娜，那真的让我笑得不行。

123
00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:28.810
I'm going to paraphrase it, but it said something like, everyone in life deserves the person they cannot sit next to in a serious situation.
我大概复述一下，大意是说，每个人生命中都应该有一个在严肃场合绝对不能坐在一起的人。

124
00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:36.950
And I just think about if I had taken that leadership class with you, we wouldn't have been able to sit next to each other.
我就在想，如果当时我和你一起上了那个领导力课程，我们肯定没法坐在一起。

125
00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:42.150
Listen, that leadership class was great because I had a really hard time finishing things.
听着，那个领导力课程很棒，因为我以前很难把事情做完。

126
00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:45.650
I would start projects all the time.
我总是会开启各种项目。

127
00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:51.730
Scripts, a one-woman show, I don't even know what, even a home project.
剧本、单人秀，我甚至不知道是什么，哪怕是家里的项目。

128
00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:58.030
I was very good at having ideas and doing the first three steps, but I wasn't a finisher.
我很擅长想点子并完成前三步，但我不是个能善始善终的人。

129
00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:03.310
And this leadership seminar was a little bit focused on finishing what you start.
而那个领导力研讨会正好侧重于如何完成你开始的事情。

130
00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:06.310
I tell you what, I finish what I start now.
我跟你说，我现在做事有始有终了。

131
00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:07.550
That's so good.
那太棒了。

132
00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:08.710
I'm all about it.
我完全支持。

133
00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:13.110
I'm like, there's got to be experts out there who can teach me skills.
我在想，外面肯定有专家能教我这些技能。

134
00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:16.350
How was I supposed to be born knowing how to do everything?
我怎么可能生来就知道该怎么做所有事呢？

135
00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:18.030
I'll go to therapy.
我会去做心理咨询。

136
00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.710
I'll go to your leadership seminar.
我会去参加你的领导力研讨会。

137
00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:20.630
Sign me up.
算我一个。

138
00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:21.970
All right.
好的。

139
00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:25.570
Fast fact number two is called awards.
第二个速览事实叫“奖项”。

140
00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:34.770
Awards because our writer, Charlie Grandy, was nominated for a Writer's Guild Award for Best Screenplay of an Episodic Comedy for this script.
之所以叫奖项，是因为我们的编剧查理·格兰迪凭借这个剧本获得了美国编剧工会奖喜剧类剧集最佳剧本提名。

141
00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:44.090
And Steve Carell used this episode as his submission for the Emmys for Outstanding Lead Actor in a Comedy Series.
而且史蒂夫·卡瑞尔用这一集作为他角逐艾美奖喜剧类剧集最佳男主角的申报作品。

142
00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:48.070
Well, I know Steve didn't win, which, remember, I was so mad about.
嗯，我知道史蒂夫没获奖，还记得吗，我当时气坏了。

143
00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:50.930
I think I said a profanity and there was a camera on us.
我想我当时说了句脏话，而且当时摄像机正对着我们。

144
00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:54.110
Steve never won an Emmy for his portrayal of Michael Scott.
史蒂夫从未凭借迈克尔·斯科特这个角色获得过艾美奖。

145
00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:56.590
How is that sentence a thing I can say?
这句话怎么可能是我能说出来的呢？

146
00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:57.210
I don't know.
我不知道。

147
00:07:57.370 --> 00:07:58.270
Wait, did Charlie win?
等等，查理获奖了吗？

148
00:07:58.410 --> 00:07:59.470
Charlie didn't win either.
查理也没获奖。

149
00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:00.530
Well, son of a...
好吧，真是个……

150
00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:01.130
I know.
我知道。

151
00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:02.590
You know, it's crazy.
你知道吗，这太疯狂了。

152
00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:06.130
That got me thinking about how you have to submit yourself for awards.
这让我想到，你必须得自己去报名参选奖项。

153
00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:08.370
I feel like that's something not a lot of people know.
我觉得很多人都不知道这一点。

154
00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:09.750
We did.
我们当时就是这么做的。

155
00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:11.510
Yeah, and it costs money.
没错，而且还要花钱。

156
00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:15.170
Sometimes the network will pay for it.
有时候电视网会帮你出这笔钱。

157
00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:15.470
Right.
对。

158
00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:23.030
But I think as an actor, you pick one or two episodes that you think best represent your performance.
但我觉得作为演员，你会挑选一两集你认为最能代表你表演水平的剧集。

159
00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.130
You also pick your category.
你还要选择你的奖项类别。

160
00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:30.250
You can decide if you want to be submitted for lead or supporting.
你可以决定是报名角逐主角还是配角。

161
00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:31.070
I know.
我知道。

162
00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:34.690
I was very excited because season four of The Office,
我当时特别兴奋，因为《办公室》第四季的时候，

163
00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:41.170
there was an article that said I was a dark horse for a nomination for supporting actress in a comedy
有一篇文章说我是喜剧类最佳女配角提名的黑马，

164
00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:45.650
because I had that whole great, you know, arc with sprinkles and all of that.
因为我当时有那段关于斯普林克尔斯（Sprinkles）的精彩剧情线，诸如此类的。

165
00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:46.130
Yes.
是的。

166
00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:47.670
And I was so excited.
我当时太激动了。

167
00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:49.350
That was enough for me.
对我来说这就足够了。

168
00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:55.030
I mean, I realized that like some people were able to have sort of a big machine behind them
我是说，我意识到有些人背后有强大的团队支持，

169
00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:55.630
campaigning.
为他们进行竞选宣传。

170
00:08:55.850 --> 00:09:00.990
But just the fact that this one person saw my performance, I cut out the magazine.
但仅仅因为有这么一个人认可了我的表演，我就把那本杂志剪了下来。

171
00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:01.650
I saved it.
我把它珍藏了起来。

172
00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:03.290
I totally get that, Ange.
我完全理解，安吉。

173
00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:06.750
I was only nominated the one year for season two.
我只在第二季那年获得过一次提名。

174
00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:07.230
Yeah.
是啊。

175
00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:11.750
And occasionally, though, my name would come up on those lists.
不过偶尔，我的名字也会出现在那些名单上。

176
00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:13.190
Yes, it's so exciting.
没错，那真的太令人兴奋了。

177
00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:13.570
Yeah.
是啊。

178
00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:15.190
What's your fast fact number three?
你的第三个速览事实是什么？

179
00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:18.930
Fast fact number three is called Time to Make the Donuts.
第三个速览事实叫“该做甜甜圈了”。

180
00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:20.050
Do I get a donut?
我有甜甜圈吃吗？

181
00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:21.370
Did you make donuts?
你做了甜甜圈吗？

182
00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:23.390
No, I wish I had, though.
没有，但我真希望我做了。

183
00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:24.510
I know you love a donut.
我知道你很爱吃甜甜圈。

184
00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:25.190
I do.
我确实爱吃。

185
00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:29.130
I should have known I can't say donut and not provide you with a donut.
我早该想到，我不该提甜甜圈却不给你准备一个。

186
00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.790
You literally said it's time to make the donuts.
你刚才明明说了“该做甜甜圈了”。

187
00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:33.010
Oh, no.
噢，不。

188
00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.190
This is not a scotch and splenda.
这可不是苏格兰威士忌加代糖。

189
00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:35.810
I know.
我知道。

190
00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:38.470
You got me thinking it's a scotch and splenda moment.
你让我以为这是个喝苏格兰威士忌加代糖的时刻。

191
00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:45.050
No, this is a little mini deep dive that I found incredibly charming based on the fact
不，这是一个小小的深度挖掘，我觉得非常迷人，因为迈克尔说了那句“该做甜甜圈了”。

192
00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.990
that Michael says it's time to make the donuts.
就是那句台词。

193
00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:49.490
Oh, the expression.
噢，那个表达方式。

194
00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:50.350
Yes.
是的。

195
00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:52.590
Do you remember that commercial?
你还记得那个广告吗？

196
00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:54.610
It was from the 80s.
那是80年代的广告。

197
00:09:54.710 --> 00:10:00.550
It actually ran from 1981 all the way to 1997, and it was for Dunkin' Donuts.
它实际上从1981年一直播到了1997年，是为唐恩都乐（Dunkin' Donuts）做的广告。

198
00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:02.170
Do you remember it?
你还记得吗？

199
00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:04.430
Well, I was overseas until 84.
嗯，我在84年之前都在国外。

200
00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:05.430
Okay.
好吧。

201
00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:09.170
This, by the way, is a thing my husband loves to tease me about.
顺便提一下，我丈夫特别喜欢拿这件事取笑我。

202
00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:13.490
He said, Ange, you can't say you were in Indonesia for everything, okay?
他说，安吉，你不能什么事都说你在印尼，好吗？

203
00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:17.390
But there's some pop culture that's lost on you.
但有些流行文化你确实没赶上。

204
00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:17.890
Yes.
是的。

205
00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:18.730
Okay.
好吧。

206
00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:20.590
Well, I grew up without television.
我从小家里没有电视。

207
00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:21.370
Isn't that crazy?
这不是很疯狂吗？

208
00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:22.930
Until I was a teenager.
直到我十几岁的时候。

209
00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:24.010
Wow.
哇。

210
00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:24.690
I know.
我知道。

211
00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:26.850
And then you wound up on television.
然后你最后竟然上了电视。

212
00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:29.110
And then I was obsessed with television.
之后我就对电视着了迷。

213
00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:29.850
I bet.
我敢肯定。

214
00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:34.110
It's like the kid that, like, their parents don't let them have any sweets, and then they
就像那些父母不让吃糖的孩子，

215
00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:35.770
come to your house and they raid your pantry.
他们到你家就会把你的储藏室洗劫一空。

216
00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:36.430
Yeah.
没错。

217
00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.730
I became, like, obsessed with television.
我变得对电视非常痴迷。

218
00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:40.410
Well, let me bring you up to speed.
好吧，让我来给你补补课。

219
00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:40.750
Okay.
好的。

220
00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:48.570
Fred the baker was a character played by actor Michael Vail in these Dunkin' Donut commercials.
面包师弗雷德是演员迈克尔·维尔在这些唐恩都乐广告中饰演的角色。

221
00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:49.150
Okay.
好的。

222
00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:52.490
And he was known for his catchphrase, time to make the donuts.
他以那句口头禅“该做甜甜圈了”而闻名。

223
00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:59.670
So it would just be him, like, all day coming in and out of his door saying, time to make
所以他整天就是进进出出他的门，说着“该做

224
